def shuffle_quiz_options(options: list[str], correct_idx: int):
    """
    FIX (Bug 1): QUIZ_QUESTIONS was authored with the correct answer almost
    always sitting at option A or B (out of the original 200 questions: 141
    at index 0, 50 at index 1, 9 at index 2, and literally ZERO at index 3)
    — every quiz delivery path (recurring group poll, DM /quiz, group quiz
    giveaway) then sent `options` straight through in that exact stored
    order. That is why players noticed the answer almost always landing in
    option 1 or 2 — it really was, on nearly every question, regardless of
    which one came up. Call this right before sending ANY quiz question to
    actually randomize where the correct answer lands (uniformly across all
    4 slots), instead of trusting the source data order. Returns
    (shuffled_options, new_correct_idx) — callers MUST use the RETURNED
    correct_idx for correct_option_id / answer-checking, not the original
    one, since the correct answer's position changes on every call.
    """
    indices = list(range(len(options)))
    random.shuffle(indices)
    shuffled_options = [options[i] for i in indices]
    new_correct_idx = indices.index(correct_idx)
    return shuffled_options, new_correct_idx


